Yes, but only via package-level state, not a per-Command-instance mechanism. The response's evidence shows cobra.go declares two package-level variable slices:

    var initializers []func()   (cobra.go:42, e3/e33)
    var finalizers []func()     (cobra.go:43, e1/e31)

and two package functions that append to them:

    func OnInitialize(y ...func()) {
        initializers = append(initializers, y...)
    }   (cobra.go:99-101, e36)

    func OnFinalize(y ...func()) {
        finalizers = append(finalizers, y...)
    }   (cobra.go:105-107, e37)

So the way to "share" something across cobra usage is to register closures via OnInitialize/OnFinalize, which get stored in these shared package-level slices and are presumably run around each command's Execute (per the doc comments "run when each command's Execute method is called" / "terminated"). Because initializers/finalizers are package-level vars (not fields on the Command struct), anything captured in the closures you pass to OnInitialize/OnFinalize is effectively shared globally across all commands built with this package, not scoped to one particular *cobra.Command instance.

The response does not contain any evidence of a Command-instance-scoped field (e.g., an Annotations map, Context, or similar) used for sharing variables between a single command and its subcommands/hooks — only the package-level initializers/finalizers slices and the OnInitialize/OnFinalize functions are shown (cobra.go:42-43, 99-107). The related preRun/postRun methods (command.go:1015-1022, e10/e11) appear in the "related" list but no snippet/body for them is included in the response, so I cannot say whether they read shared instance state.
